// Fixture: indentation_mixed_tabs_spaces_error
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §3 -- "Only one type of indentation character can be
// used inside a single data structure's scope. If more than one type of indentation
// character is found inside a data structure definition / instantiation, the tool must
// generate an error." Negative-path companion to the two positive fixtures. This file
// mixes a tab-indented statement and a space-indented statement within the SAME scope
// (Creature's field list) -- not across different, independently-indented scopes, which
// would not violate the rule.
//
// The mixing is deliberate and exact: `hitpoints` below is indented with one literal
// tab character; `mana`, in the same scope, is indented with four literal space
// characters instead. (This distinction is invisible in a typical text viewer -- the
// raw bytes are what matter here, not visual alignment.)

define Creature
	hitpoints = u32
    mana = u32

// ERROR: `hitpoints` above is tab-indented; `mana` is space-indented; both are in
// Creature's same top-level scope. This must be a compile error, not a warning and
// not silently tolerated by treating tabs and spaces as visually equivalent.

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Compile error: mixed indentation characters within a single scope (Creature's
//   field-list scope), reported at phase 3 (Parse) per §12 -- this is caught during
//   parsing/indentation-structure analysis, before any type registration or resolution
//   would even begin.
